 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Reconstruction Difficulty:** Reconstructing NeRFs from sparse views without a learned prior is exceptionally difficult, limiting training to dense-view settings [S2].
-*   **Two-Stage Dependency:** Traditional LDM for 3D relies on a two-stage training scheme (auto-decoder first, then LDM), which can be unstable or inefficient compared to single-stage approaches [S2].
+*   **Precision:** The sources do not specify precision requirements. [G] Standard practice suggests using FP16 or BF16 for training to save memory, but FP32 may be needed for stability in the denoising network.
+*   **Boundaries:** The noise schedule functions $\alpha^{(t)}$ and $\sigma^{(t)}$ must be well-defined for $t \in [0, T]$. [G] Common schedules include linear or cosine schedules.
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If the auto-decoder fails to converge, the extracted latent codes $x_i$ may not form a valid distribution for the diffusion model. [G] Careful validation of the auto-decoder is required.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Latent Diffusion Models (LDM)
-A general framework that performs diffusion in a latent space, significantly improving computational efficiency and output quality compared to pixel-space diffusion [S1]. It leverages pre-trained conditional models for stable and efficient synthesis based on text prompts or other conditional inputs (e.g., Stable Diffusion) [S1]. Variants like ControlNet add conditions (canny edge, pose, depth) for fine-grained control, while LoRA allows efficient fine-tuning with minimal overhead [S1].
+### [Latent Diffusion Models (LDM)](14900-latent-diffusion-models-ldm.md)
+Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) perform the diffusion process in a compressed latent space rather than the high-dimensional pixel space, significantly improving computational efficiency and output quality. In the context of 3D volume rendering, hierarchical LDMs define a generative model over latent variables representing scene geometry, camera trajectories, and fine details, enabling the synthesis of voxel-based neural fields. This approach sets it apart from standard Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) by decoupling the diffusion process from the expensive decoding step, allowing for stable and efficient generation of complex 3D scenes conditioned on hierarchical inputs.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Significantly faster than direct 3D diffusion due to the reduced dimensionality of the latent space [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Lower memory footprint as the diffusion process operates on compact latent codes rather than full 3D volumes or large NeRF weights [S1, S2].
-*   **Strengths:** High-quality output, computational efficiency, ability to leverage powerful 2D diffusion priors [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-training of an auto-decoder (in two-stage methods), which may limit flexibility [S2]. Reconstruction from sparse views is challenging without strong priors [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When generating high-quality 3D assets efficiently, especially when leveraging existing 2D diffusion models or when computational resources are limited relative to the 3D resolution [S1, S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When direct 3D generation without a latent prior is required, or when the scene cannot be effectively compressed into a latent space [S2].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Significantly more efficient than DDPMs in pixel space due to the reduced dimensionality of the latent space [S1]. The exact complexity depends on the latent representation (e.g., triplane size) and the denoising network architecture.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Scales better with resolution than pixel-space diffusion models because the diffusion process operates on a fixed-size latent representation regardless of the output resolution [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High computational efficiency, improved output quality, ability to handle complex 3D scenes, and support for conditional generation (e.g., text, single-view) [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained auto-decoder, which adds complexity to the training pipeline. The quality of the generated 3D scenes is dependent on the expressiveness of the latent representation [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** When generating high-quality 3D scenes is required, and computational resources are limited compared to pixel-space diffusion. Also suitable for tasks requiring conditional generation or reconstruction from sparse views [S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the scene geometry is extremely simple and can be generated efficiently with DDPMs, or when a pre-trained auto-decoder is not available and training one is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-Latent Diffusion Models were introduced to address the computational bottlenecks of applying diffusion models directly to high-dimensional data [S1]. In the context of 3D, they have been adapted to generate neural fields and other 3D representations by learning priors over latent codes [S2]. The approach has evolved from two-stage training schemes (auto-decoder + LDM) to more unified single-stage approaches like SSDNeRF, which jointly learns the triplane features, NeRF decoder, and diffusion prior [S2].
+Latent Diffusion Models (LDMs) were introduced to address the computational inefficiency of DDPMs in high-dimensional spaces [S1]. In the context of 3D, LDMs have been adapted to generate neural fields (e.g., NeRFs) by learning a prior distribution over latent codes [S2]. The Single-Stage Diffusion NeRF (SSDNeRF) framework is a notable example that unifies 3D generation and reconstruction by jointly learning a triplane NeRF auto-decoder and a triplane latent diffusion prior [S2].
 
